which controller, and which install script
------------------------------------------

Which controllers a kernel supports depends on the kernel, and it
changed.  con.c in this tree - which is 1.61, main.c prints the version -
has HDCA gone:

    struct biovec biosw[] = {
        &nodev, &nulldev, &nulldev,         /* 0 = no device */
        &nodev, &nodev, &nodev,             /* 1 = HDCA - removed, obsolete */
        &djopen, &djclose, &djstrat,        /* 2 = DJ-DMA */
        &mwopen, &mwclose, &mwstrat,        /* 3 = HD-DMA */
    };

but 1.4 still had it, and 1.6 is where it was retired.  Do not read the
table above as describing every kernel in kernels/.

The boot banner tells you which one you are running, because main.c
prints only the devices that have a strategy routine:

    pr("disks: ");
    for (i = 1; i < nbdev; i++)
        if (biosw[i].strat != &nodev)
            pr("%s ", devname[i]);

so a 1.4 kernel says

    disks: hdca(rev4)  djdma  hddma

and a 1.61 kernel, with HDCA nodev, says only djdma and hddma.  If
hdca(rev4) is in the list the kernel can use it.

So on 1.4 all four install scripts are live, and the standalone signon
is the guide - it is also the one that gets the controllers the right
way round, where DRIVE_A's README has them swapped:

    m5init, m10init, m16init    HDDMA
    hdinit                      HDCA   (1.4 only; gone by 1.6)


the 1.4 source is gone
----------------------

There is no source for 1.4 anywhere and there will not be.  src/micronix
is 1.61, and the HDCA driver is not in it - so kernels/micronix.14 is the
only surviving copy of that driver, as a binary.

It is not a total loss, because the symbol table survived beside it.
kernels/micronix.14.sym names the whole driver:

    0xaf88 _hdopen     0xb189 _hdstrat    0xb107 _hdclose
    0xaeb3 _hdint      0xb570 _hdsint     0xade8 __hdproc
    0xae2c __hdrhdr    0xaee6 __hdrdat    0xaf39 __hdwdat
    0xae3f __hdup

and micronix.161.sym has none of them, which is the retirement visible
from the outside.  _hdstrat is the routine main.c tests against &nodev
before printing a device, so that symbol existing at 0xb189 is the same
fact as hdca(rev4) appearing in the 1.4 boot banner.

A disassembly with those labels is therefore a way back to how HDCA was
actually driven; kernels/micronix.167.dis shows the technique has been
used on this tree before.  The board itself is documented independently
of the software - see below - so a lost driver is not a lost controller.


read the manuals first
----------------------

Both controllers have their technical references in extra/docs, and
every device has a man page in filesystem/usr/man/man4.  They answer
most of what this file was written to work out, and they outrank
hwsim/d1/*.c, which are behavioural models of the hardware and no better
than the reading behind them.

    extra/docs/HDCA_Controller_Technical_Reference_Rev2_Feb83.pdf
    extra/docs/HDC_DMA_Technical_Manual_1983.pdf
    extra/docs/HDCDMA_Disk_Controller_Rev1_Apr82.pdf
    extra/docs/HD-DMA_Preliminary_Technical_Manual_Jan82.pdf
    extra/docs/MPZ80_CPU_Technical_Manual_Rev_1_Apr82.pdf
    extra/docs/DJ_DMA_Technical_Manual_Revision_1_Apr82.pdf
    extra/docs/Micronix_Operating_System_1.61_Jun83.pdf
    extra/hardware/djdma/Morrow/decision/pcb_pics/HDCA-4A_*.jpg

    filesystem/usr/man/man4/{hdca,hddma,djdma,multio,io,mem,null}.4

What the man pages settle:

  Formatting is a CP/M job by design, not by accident.  Both hdca.4 and
  hddma.4 say "Micronix does not support a disk formatting program, so
  each drive must be formatted under CP/M."  That is why every route to
  a Micronix hard disk goes through a CP/M boot disk first.

  Skew does not matter.  "A sector skew of 6 is recommended, but is not
  critical.  Since these drives are soft-sectored, there is no
  compatibility problem with different skews."  So FORMATMW's default of
  3 is fine and skew is never the explanation for a disk that will not
  read - look at the sector size instead.

  The two controllers take different drives.  HDCA is up to four 8 inch
  Winchesters, m10, m20 or m26, named hda through hdd at major 1, minor
  0-3, and Micronix detects their size by itself.  HDDMA is up to four
  5 1/4 inch drives, m5, m10 or m16, named m5a through m16d at major 3,
  with the size encoded in the minor: m5 is 0-3, m10 is 4-7, m16 is 8-11.
  HDDMA has no autodetect, which is why the size is in the device name.

  Micronix rolls the tracks.  From hddma.4: "Micronix logically 'rolls'
  the tracks by (number of tracks) / 2.  Thus on an m5, which has 153
  tracks, logical track 0 is physical track 76."

  The reason is worth having, because it says which way to unroll.  One
  hard disk usually serves as both root and swap, and when rootdev ==
  swapdev Micronix puts swap in the space above the file system.  So the
  logical disk is

       ilist           file space             swap space
      |-------|-----------------------------|------------|

  and rolling by half makes the physical disk

       file space   swap space   ilist     file space
      ------------|------------|-------|-----------------

  which brings the ilist and the swap space next to each other in the
  middle of the platter.  The heads spend most of a busy system's time
  over exactly those two, so the seek between them becomes short.

  Anything reading a drive image directly is looking at physical tracks
  and has to undo this - including the python that went looking for
  STAT.COM while this file was being written, which only got away with
  it because that disk was CP/M's and CP/M does not roll anything.


and why the mismatch is an HDDMA problem only
---------------------------------------------

HDCA cannot get this wrong.  It has one sector size, it is 512, and 512
is what Micronix wants - so a drive FORMATHD prepared is already the
size the kernel expects, and there is no argument to get wrong.

The hazard belongs entirely to HDDMA, and it is the price of that
controller being the programmable one.  FORMATMW will happily give you
any of five sector sizes, only one of which Micronix can read, and the
default is not it.


booting a hard disk, and why nothing does
-----------------------------------------

mon447 can boot either hard disk controller.  nuboot resets the HDDMA on
port 54, homes with ffff step pulses, reads cylinder 0 head 0 sector 1
to physical 0100 and enters task 1 there; boothd does the same for the
HDCA.  hddma.c answers all of it - the ports, and the command set 0
read, 1 write, 2 readhead, 3 format, 4 specify, 5 sense, 6 nop, which is
also what BOOTMW's equates and the kernel's mw.c use.  So the ROM path
works.  What is missing is anything to find at the other end.

It is a Micronix path, not a CP/M one, and the ROM says so itself: the
sector size it loads into the controller is "secsiz equ 3 ;512 byte
sectors for Micronix", which is the same 3 the kernel's mw.c sends.
BOOTMW's Cbios uses 7, a thousand and twenty four, so a CP/M hard disk
cannot be read by this boot at all.  Whoever wrote nuboot meant it to
start Micronix off a hard disk.

Nothing writes that sector.  m5init and hdinit make a filesystem, copy
the running system across, relink /dev/root and /dev/swap and patch the
kernel; there is no step that writes a boot block, and mkfs's only
exclusion is at the end of the device, for swap, as its man page says.
The two supported ways in are on the cold boot diskette: run m5boot.com
from CP/M, or sysgen m5load onto a floppy's system tracks so RESET boots
that floppy, which then boots Micronix off the hard disk.

The reason it is done that way is arithmetic.  mw.c maps a block to a
cylinder as blk/spc plus half the cylinder count, wrapping, so block 0
is the middle cylinder.  Turn that around and physical cylinder 0 - the
one place the ROM looks - is at exactly the halfway mark of the
filesystem's block space:

	5 meg	153x4x17	block  5237 of 10404
	10 meg	306x4x17	block 10405 of 20808
	16 meg	306x6x17	block 15607 of 31212

That is ordinary data space.  Nothing reserves it and nothing can: mkfs
only knows how to exclude blocks at the end of the device.

The filesystem does have a reserved boot block, and it is no use here.
Block 0 is never allocated and reads as zero - fslib's readblk and
writeblk both special-case it, and the loader's own readblk bails on it
- and v6's mkfs will even install a boot program there.  But block 0 is
physical cylinder tracks/2, cylinder 76 on the five meg drive, and no
ROM will ever look there.  The one block that is reserved is somewhere
the hardware cannot boot from, and the one place the hardware boots from
is not reserved.

If you write a boot block to cylinder 0 anyway it will work for a while,
which is the worst way for this to fail.  Blocks are handed out from the
low end upward, so the halfway mark is not reached until the filesystem
is about half full, and then some ordinary file lands on the boot sector
and the disk stops booting with nothing to say why.

There is a way to reserve it that needs nothing new, and it is the old
unix answer: a block is in use if an inode says so.  Give a file the
blocks of cylinder 0 and the free list will never offer them again,
fsck will agree, and writing the file writes the boot area.

The block to own is not what the ROM's comment says.  rdtbl reads "sector
1, head 0, cyl 0" but the byte is db 0, and mw.c computes sector as
csec % nsecs from csec = blk % spc, counting from zero.  So the ROM's
sector 1 is the driver's sector 0, and the block is c * spc exactly with
c = tracks - tracks/2 - no plus one:

	5 meg	block  5236	cylinder 0 is 68 blocks
	10 meg	block 10404	cylinder 0 is 68 blocks
	16 meg	block 15606	cylinder 0 is 102 blocks

Taking the whole cylinder rather than one block costs 34K or 51K, keeps
the second level next to the first where it can be read without seeking,
and stops the filesystem putting anything else in cylinder 0.

The tools for it are here.  Make the file, edit its inode so its block
list is c*spc upward - mnix block -e - then icheck -s to rebuild the
free list from what the inodes claim, which both marks these blocks used
and returns the ones the file was originally given.  icheck -b <block>
says which inode holds a block, which is how to check it afterwards and
how to notice if it ever stops being true.

Give it mode 0.  Nothing is meant to read or write it through the
filesystem - the ROM reads the sector off the platter without knowing
there is a filesystem there at all - so every open is a mistake, and mode
0 turns the mistakes into errors instead of into a disk that stops
booting three months later.  It also keeps the file out of anything that
copies what it can read.

Two things mode 0 does not do, and it is worth being clear about both.
Unlink is permission on the directory, not on the file, so mode 0 does
not stop rm.  And root ignores modes entirely, which matters because that
is who installs run as.

The first of those is worth answering properly rather than by putting the
file in / and hoping.  Give it a directory of its own, in /, and give the
directory mode 0 too.  That costs two inodes and one block, and the block
comes off the ordinary free list rather than out of cylinder 0.

What it buys is a barrier against a different verb.  Mode 0 on the file
answers open.  Mode 0 on the directory answers unlink, and also creat,
which is the one that is easy to forget: without it, nothing stops a
file of the same name being made over the top of this one, which loses
the blocks just as thoroughly as rm would and looks less like a mistake
while it is happening.  Removing the directory itself needs write on /,
so that is root only as well.

The layers, then, are: to lose the boot area you have to get past mode 0
on the file, mode 0 on the directory, and write permission on / - and
each of those is a separate deliberate act rather than a slip.  ncheck
will still report the path, so it stays findable by the tools that check
filesystems even though nothing can walk to it.

installing it
-------------

    mkdir /boot
    touch /boot/bootmw
    sync
    umount the filesystem
    patch the inode on the raw device: block list c*spc upward, size spc*512
    icheck -s to rebuild the free list
    mount the filesystem
    open /boot/bootmw and write the boot area through it
    sync, umount, chmod 0 the file and the directory

Three things about that order are load bearing.

The inode has to be patched while the filesystem is unmounted.  A mounted
kernel holds that inode in core, and writing the raw device underneath it
achieves nothing - the cached copy is written back over the patch at
umount, and the failure is silent.  That is also why the mount has to
come after: the newly mounted filesystem reads the inode off the disk
rather than believing what it remembers.

The free list has to be rebuilt.  touch makes a file with no blocks, so
filling in a block list claims blocks that are still on the free list -
they belong to the file and are also queued to be handed out, and the
next thing that allocates gets one.  icheck -s rebuilds the list from
what the inodes say, which claims cylinder 0 and costs nothing else.
fsck alone will also do it; it knows both halves of this by name,
"allocated blocks in the free list" and "blocks missing from the free
list".

The size has to be set, to spc * 512.  An inode with a block list and a
zero size is an empty file: reads return nothing, and a write goes
through the allocator rather than to the blocks that are sitting there.

Then writing the file writes the boot area, because the kernel maps
block c*spc through the same mw.c arithmetic the ROM's read uses and
lands on cylinder 0 head 0 sector 0.  That is the whole point of doing it
this way rather than writing the platter directly: one path, one mapping,
and no second opinion about where the sector is.

what goes in the first 512 bytes
--------------------------------

The ROM reads one sector to physical 0100 and enters task 1 at 0100, so
the first block of the file is a first level boot and has to be built for
0100 - not 0080, where the floppy's runs, because that address belongs to
the DJ/DMA ROM's convention and not to this one.  M5LOAD's first level
cannot be dropped in unchanged for that reason.

M5LOAD is 12K, which is 24 blocks - three times the eight addresses an
inode holds, so the boot file is an ILARG file with an indirect block
whatever else is decided.  It is a third of cylinder 0 on the five and
ten meg drives and a quarter of it on the sixteen, so the cylinder holds
it with room to spare.

Not all of that is program.  M5LOAD is a floppy's system tracks, so it is
zero to 0800, the first level at 0800, the second level from 0a00 to
6272, and padding after - about nine blocks of content in a 24 block
file.  Which means it cannot be written into the boot file as it stands:
block 0 of the file is what the ROM reads, and block 0 of M5LOAD is zero.
The two pieces have to be laid down deliberately, first level in block 0
and second level from block 1, and the first level is being rewritten for
0100 anyway.

It has 512 bytes to work in rather than the floppy's 128, and it has less
to do than the floppy's does: nuboot has already sent the controller its
load constants command by the time this code runs, so the drive is
configured and only read commands are needed.  It has to pull the rest of
cylinder 0 in and enter the second level, which is M5LOAD's, at 0100 -
so it has to load it somewhere else and jump, or move itself first.

What this does not survive: deleting the file, or reinstalling with
cptree.  cptree copies contents, not block numbers, and running as root
it will copy this file happily - leaving a new disk with a boot file that
has the right bytes in the wrong blocks and does not boot.  That failure
looks like a successful install, so it is the one to write down.  The
numbers are also per drive type, so a file built for the five meg drive
is wrong on the other two.


where the drive files live
--------------------------

Controllers name units hddma-0..3 and hdca-0..3.  drive_open resolves
those in the directory given by -d, or the current one if there is no
-d.  It opens O_CREAT, so a name it cannot find is created blank rather
than refused: if a run seems to have lost its disk, check -d before
looking anywhere else.

what a simulated drive file actually is
---------------------------------------

Not a disk image in the sense the floppies are.  Two differences, and
both of them will silently give you a disk that does not boot.

There is a label first: 2048 bytes, holding the magic d15cc0de, the
sector size, and the cylinders, heads and sectors per track.  lib/harddisk.c
writes one when it creates a drive it cannot find, with the sector size
at its 2048 byte default and the geometry zero, and grows the geometry
as the drive gets used.  Data starts at 2048.

And the data is in physical order - cylinder, then head, then sector -
which is not the order a filesystem's blocks are in, because mw.c rotates
by half the cylinder count.  Filesystem block B is at

	cyl  = (B/spc + tracks/2) mod tracks
	head = (B mod spc) / spt
	sec  = (B mod spc) mod spt

	physical sector = (cyl * heads + head) * spt + sec

which for the five meg drive puts block 5236 at physical sector 0 and
block 0 at physical sector 5168.  A filesystem written straight into a
drive file is not wrong by an offset, it is shuffled, and the only thing
that will read it is nothing.

This is also why mkfs seeking to 512 * blkno is right on Micronix and
would be wrong here.  There it is talking to /dev/m5a and the kernel does
the rotation on the way past; a host tool writing the drive file has to
do it itself.

booting one, which works
------------------------

Done end to end in the simulator, off a hand built filesystem:

    make bootimg in micronix/stand	first level padded to a block,
					second level after it
    mkfs -i bootimg <fs>		claims cylinder 0 as it builds the
					free list, so nothing else is needed
    permute the filesystem into physical order, prepend a label
    d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -c 0x0c -d <dir>

-c 0x0c is SW_HDDMA and SW_NOMON, which is what the monitor's switch test
wants for nuboot; 0x14 is the floppy, and it is the default.

    Micronix loader for the HD-DMA
    Files:
    No bootable files

which is the whole path: nuboot read cylinder 0 head 0 sector 0, entered
mwboot1 at 0100, which moved itself to c000, read the eight sectors after
it through the channel the rom had already set up, and jumped to the
second level - which found the drive, read the filesystem and listed the
root directory.  Nothing bootable in it because that filesystem is empty
apart from /boot, which is mode 0.

What is left is the part that needs the simulator rather than the host:
m5init on a real drive from a booted 1.67, with its mkfs given -i, and
the same path should find a kernel to load.

To replace a boot after the fact, dd over the blocks the file already
owns.  Nothing about the filesystem changes - the inode is what protects
them, and it goes on claiming them whatever they contain.


