booting the 1.6 standalone, and what stops it
---------------------------------------------

Two things had to be true before the standalone floppy would run its
init, and one of them is still open.

The first was the hard sectored bit - see MAPPING, which is the whole
story.  Without it every filesystem read landed two cylinders low and
/etc/init read as a sticky directory.  With it the kernel mounts root,
init runs, and the standalone prints its welcome telling you to source
m5init.

The second is swap, and this one is worth knowing before it wastes an
afternoon.  The standalone ships with

	root dev: djdma/12
	swap dev: nodev/0

and the console then says "No more space on disk nodev/0" and nothing
more happens.  That is balloc failing on device zero: the kernel wanted
to swap and there is no swap device.  Patching the swap word in the
kernel image - it is the second of the two device words, immediately
after the root device, and they are the words m5init patches with
"ddt /b/micronix sB6CB 0 3 0 3" - to a spare hard drive gets past it:

	swap dev: hddma/1

after which the welcome appears every time and init gets as far as
reading /etc/ttys and stat'ing the terminals in it.

Why it needs to swap at all is the open question, and the reason to be
careful about calling that patch a fix.  The disk shipped with no swap
device, so on real hardware it evidently did not need one; a machine with
1024K and 64K tasks has room for sixteen processes and this is running
two.  Something about how core is being sized or handed out here differs
from the real machine, and the patch papers over it.

Where it stops now: init reads /etc/ttys, stats /dev/ttyA through ttyI
growing its break as it builds the table, and then traps to the rom
monitor - the ":" prompt with "?" for every character you type is
mon447's, at line 1326 and 1494 of its source, not a shell.  Only ttyA
has a login entry in /etc/ttys, so init has not yet reached the point of
spawning one.

Note also that writes go to a -delta file beside the image and persist
between runs, so a run that gets further than the last one may only be
inheriting its mess.  Clear the deltas before drawing conclusions.

